Àmútọ̀runwá (names tied to birth circumstance): Táíwò and Kẹ́hìndé for twins, Ìdòwú for the child after twins, Àlàbá for the next, Dàda for a child born with locked hair, Ìgè for a breech birth.

Orúkọ ìdílé (family names) often begin with a lineage or title element, such as Adé- (crown) or Ọlá- (wealth).

Oríkì are praise poems for a person, lineage or town.$v$, $v$Ilẹ̀ Abínibí Atlas prototype$v$, $v$First Ilẹ̀ Abínibí Atlas prototype (pilot dataset). Source citations and community review still to be added.$v$, 'under_review'::evidence_status, 'published'::publish_status, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;

