The child who is not taught will sell the family house. Proverbs are treated as the “palm oil” that helps words go down, and elders use them to teach.$v$, null, null, $v$Abínibí$v$, 'under_review'::evidence_status, $v$Ilẹ̀ Abínibí Atlas prototype$v$, 'published'::publish_status) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
12th–15th centuries…$v$, $v$Origins [Oral tradition]: Abínibí tradition places the beginning of the Abínibí at Ilé-Ifẹ̀ with Odùduwà. Accounts differ on dates and details.
12th–15th centuries [Documented]: Ifẹ̀ produces naturalistic bronze and terracotta heads.
17th–18th centuries [Documented]: The Ọ̀yọ́ Empire grows under the Aláàfin and dominates much of the region.
Early 19th century [Documented]: Ọ̀yọ́ declines. New centres such as Ìbàdàn and Abẹ́òkúta emerge from the wars and migrations of the 1820s–30s.
1807 onward [Documented]: Freed Abínibí captives are resettled in Freetown. Their descendants are known as Aku.
19th century [Documented]: Large numbers of Abínibí-speaking captives are carried to Bahia, Pernambuco and Cuba. They are called Nagô in Brazil and Lucumí in Cuba.
Mid to late 19th century [Documented]: Some Brazilian and Cuban returnees settle in Lagos, Ouidah and Porto-Novo. In Lagos the descendants are known as Àgùdà.
1970 [Documented]: Oyotunji African Village is founded in South Carolina.
2005 [Documented]: Ọ̀ṣun-Òṣogbo Sacred Grove becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site.$v$, null, $v$Origins to 2005$v$, null, 'under_review'::evidence_status, $v$Ilẹ̀ Abínibí Atlas prototype$v$, 'published'::publish_status) on conflict (slug) do nothing;



